@page
@model VeSessionManager.Web.Pages.Account.EnableAuthenticatorModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Set up two-factor authentication";
    Layout = "_AppLayout";
}

<div class="page-head">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow"><a asp-page="./TwoFactor">Two-factor authentication</a></p>
        <h1>Set up</h1>
    </div>
</div>

@if (Model.ErrorMessage is not null)
{
    <div class="vesm-alert error">@Model.ErrorMessage</div>
}

<div class="session-panel" style="display:block;max-width:640px;">
    <ol class="setup-steps">
        <li>
            <strong>Open your authenticator</strong> — a password manager with TOTP support (Bitwarden,
            1Password) or a dedicated app (Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, Authy).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Add this account</strong>, whichever way suits the tool you use.

            @*
                Three ways, and the paste-the-URI one leads deliberately.

                The page originally led with the QR and mentioned the key as a trailing line. That is
                backwards for a password manager, which wants the otpauth:// URI pasted into its TOTP
                field — it carries the issuer and account label, so the vault entry names itself, and
                it needs neither a camera nor sixteen characters typed by hand. The URI was being
                computed and fed to the QR encoder while never being shown at all.
            *@
            <div class="setup-option">
                <label for="authenticatorUri">Setup URI — paste this into a password manager</label>
                <div class="copy-row">
                    @* readonly, not disabled: a disabled input cannot be selected or copied. *@
                    <input id="authenticatorUri" class="totp-uri" type="text" readonly
                           value="@Model.AuthenticatorUri" spellcheck="false" />
                    <button type="button" class="btn-secondary btn-sm" data-copy-target="authenticatorUri">Copy</button>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="setup-option">
                <label for="authenticatorKey">Or type this key in</label>
                <div class="copy-row">
                    <input id="authenticatorKey" class="totp-key" type="text" readonly
                           value="@Model.DisplayKey" spellcheck="false" />
                    <button type="button" class="btn-secondary btn-sm" data-copy-target="authenticatorKey">Copy</button>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="setup-option">
                <span class="setup-option-label">Or scan this code</span>
                @* Inline SVG rather than an <img>: the CSP needs no exception for it, and it stays
                   crisp at any size. The white plate is not decoration — a QR is black-on-white by
                   specification, and a dark-mode page behind the quiet zone makes it unscannable. *@
                <div class="qr-holder">@Html.Raw(Model.QrCodeSvg)</div>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Enter the 6-digit code</strong> your authenticator now shows, to prove it is working.
            <form method="post" class="totp-verify">
                <div class="field" style="max-width:220px;">
                    <label asp-for="Input.Code"></label>
                    <input asp-for="Input.Code" autocomplete="one-time-code" inputmode="numeric"
                           pattern="[0-9 ]*" spellcheck="false" autofocus />
                    <span asp-validation-for="Input.Code" class="field-error"></span>
                </div>
                <button type="submit" class="btn-primary">Turn on two-factor authentication</button>
            </form>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="session-sub">
        Two-factor authentication is not switched on until a code verifies, so a mistyped key or a
        phone with the wrong clock cannot lock you out. You will be given recovery codes immediately
        afterwards — save them.
    </p>

    <p class="session-sub">
        <strong>All three of these carry the same secret.</strong> Anyone who can read this screen can
        add your account to their own authenticator, so treat the URI the way you would a password:
        paste it where it belongs and do not leave it on a shared display.
    </p>
</div>

@section Scripts {
    <partial name="_ValidationScriptsPartial" />
}
